{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfhaiJCcz4vCuEEGQDrWR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I rewrote the repair candidate as `streak_tolerance_alarm`, and it now passes the admission self-check. A test fit of 4 chains × 1000 draws had 0 divergent transitions, R-hat of 1.00 to 1.03 and bulk ESS of at least 253. The original attempt had failed with 401 divergences.\n\n**Why I didn't just fix the old model:** the original gambler's-fallacy model was essentially the same as `recency_weighted_gamblers_surprise`, which was already admitted this round. Repairing it would most likely have been rejected as a near-duplicate, so I switched to a different mechanism.\n\n**Hypothesis:** people have a tolerance for how long a run of identical flips can be before it looks too long to be chance. Each run longer than that raises a \"streak alarm\", growing smoothly with the excess, while shorter runs cost nothing. People pick the sequence with the smaller alarm. One tolerance is shared by everyone, and people differ in how strongly the alarm drives their choice. This targets two of the critique's significant findings: the stronger-than-predicted preference for the sequence with the shorter longest run, and the larger-than-predicted spread between participants.\n\n**Fixes made along the way:**\n- **Per-person tolerance didn't converge.** My first version let each person have their own tolerance. It failed twice (R-hat 1.17, then 1.53 after bounding the threshold). I moved the person-to-person differences into a log-normal sensitivity instead, so the hypothesis no longer says people differ in where their tolerance lies.\n- **NaN ELPD-LOO.** Very sensitive participants pushed `p_left` to exactly 0 or 1, which made ELPD-LOO NaN, so I clipped it to [1e-6, 1-1e-6].\n\n**Results:**\n- The fitted tolerance is about 2.7 flips.\n- The smoke-fit ELPD-LOO is about -1067, against -1260 for the incumbent `local_representativeness`. That comes from a short single-chain fit, though, and PSIS-LOO flags it as unreliable (15% of trials have high Pareto k, as is usual with per-person parameters), so the gap isn't a trustworthy comparison yet.\n- Not checked: whether its predictions differ enough from the existing models to pass the novelty gate.\n\nFiles are in `iter_0/candidate_0_repair_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":280,"cache_read_input_tokens":70714,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":280,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":729,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecd8a8e1-efdf-4049-b3e3-5eb5c3e80ce6","uuid":"691925c2-01d2-4c32-876d-466b3eeac7b0","timestamp":"2026-10-04T17:21:25.185Z","request_id":"req_011CfhaiH3uv28ecp9zHEb2C"}
